  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures and mainly dry weather \nwill continue through the upcoming week, with a few showers possible \nacross the Southern Tier at times.\n\nAn upper level trough and closed low will slowly move east across \nthe Midwest to Ohio Valley through mid-week. High pressure will \nremain across eastern Canada, bringing continued northeast flow \nacross the eastern Great Lakes region though a majority of the work \nweek. \n\nA shortwave trough will move across the Ohio Valley this evening. \nSubsidence and dry air will maintain mostly dry weather across the \nregion, however isentropic lift and deep moisture will approach the \nNY/PA line. Widely scattered showers are possible across the western \nSouthern Tier through this evening. Mid-high level cloud cover will \nspread across the forecast area tonight. Partial clearing is \nexpected to occur from the northwest late tonight. Low temperatures \nwill fall to the 40s with locations across the North Country \npossibly seeing the upper 30s. Confidence in temperatures reaching \nthe upper 30s is low, as the position of the high well to the north \nand uncertainty in cloud cover does not offer the best set-up for \nradiational cooling. With that being said, there is a non-zero \nchance of patchy frost in known cold spots across Jefferson and \nLewis counties tonight.\n\nLow-level easterly flow will increase to our south ahead of the \napproaching closed low Tuesday. A few showers along the NY/PA border \ncan't be ruled out. An upper level ridge is expected to move south \nfrom eastern Canada for the end of the work week, and widespread dry \nweather is anticipated across the forecast area. Temperatures will \ngradually increase each day but stay below normal for late \nSeptember.",
